What is an MRI System?
Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is an advanced, non-invasive medical imaging modality that employs a powerful magnetic field and radiofrequency pulses to generate highly detailed, cross-sectional images of the body's internal structures. Unlike computed tomography (CT) scans or traditional X-rays, MRI systems do not utilize ionizing radiation, which substantially improves patient safety, particularly for those requiring repeated scans. Its exceptional ability to differentiate soft tissues makes it indispensable for diagnosing complex conditions across neurology, orthopedics, and cardiology. The technology's reliance on magnetic fields allows for superior visualization of anatomical details, which is why it has become a cornerstone of modern diagnostic medicine.

